BRICS’ 🇮🇳: The Soft Center Between A Rock (🇺🇸) & A Hard Place (🇨🇳)

The Mirage: ‘We Have To Work And Fight Together As One’
In an article* on de-dollarization dated  22nd Jan 2026 I said: “It is not just the insult and humiliation that PM Modi and India, as a country, have to bear. It’s the realization that unless Modi Govt gives in to all or most of Trump’s demands especially on oil imports, abandoning local currency use for international payments, and opening the agri sector, Trump would continue to damage India’s economy, and possibly target those portions of India’s digital and critical infrastructures that are dependent on US origin technologies. Resistance to Trump’s demands is essential for India's interest and for PM Modi's. He must open, if not already done, all channels of communication within BRICS countries, especially with Russia and China, for mutual technological support and for a common united front…The BJP claims that Modi is a leader who commands global respect and admiration, and that when he speaks, the world listens. In his passionate address on 9th July 2015 to the Plenary Session of the 7th BRICS Summit at Ufa in the Russian Federation, PM Modi said: ‘We have to work and fight together as one’. The time has come to redeem that pledge.”

The Question Of Primacy of US$
“Xi Jinping of China, Vladimir Putin of Russia, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva of Brazil, Narendra Modi of India, Cyril Ramaphosa of South Africa, Ayatollah Ali Khamenei of Iran, Nicholas Maduro of Venezuela, Joko Widodo of Indonesia, President Abdel Fattah el-Sisi of Egypt, President Taye A Selassie of Ethiopia, and President Sheikh Mohamed bin Zayed Al Nahyan of UAE have one thing in common. They are all BRICS countries’ leaders except Venezuela which also was a ‘BRICS Partner’ country. They supported and conducted sizeable trade in non-US$ local currencies. At some point all were threatened with 100% tariffs by President Trump. No matter how Trump presents the upcoming peace deal with Iran, the end of the War will be a severe setback to Trump’s America. Iranian oil will outsell both US as well as Venezuelan crude in Asian and European markets both on account of much lower FOB price and lower freight but also because of Iran’s capacity to sell oil and gas at discounted prices in local currencies. There will be greater erosion of US$ as the major global reserve currency as more and more countries embrace de-dollarization through greater use of local currency in international trade. For this reason alone the expected upcoming peace deal between US and Iran is only an interlude …hostilities should re-ignite on the forever available nuclear enrichment issue unless Iran agrees to sell it’s oil in US$.”** My Article on X dated 15th June '26.

The Wages Of Technological Weakness
In conclusion I said:
“Expectations From Modi.
If only we were technologically stronger than what we claim, Modi ji could have taken a hard line towards Trump’s thuggery. But as things stand Modi ji is expected to ride out the humiliation of attacks on three oil tankers that had Indian crew of whom three were killed…Unlike Russia and China, Modi ji will not defy US sanctions against Iran to buy Iranian crude in local currency. Even as the Abraham Accord(s), and it’s broader appeal, is unravelling due to the unprecedented and partly successful Iranian challenge to US and Israeli hegemony in the Middle East, Modi Govt would continue to lean towards it’s I2U2 plank. In his 25th Feb 2026 address to the Israeli Knesset Modi ji said: "India stands with Israel firmly, with full conviction, in this moment and beyond". Modi ji will maintain that position. He will continue to purchase Venezuelan crude in US$ which will strengthen the petro-dollar regime. Simultaneously, Modi Govt may also ramp up existing lines of imports from China and Russia. In trade he is expected to tread the middle path of equivalence. At the upcoming BRICS Summit in Delhi, Modi ji is not expected to voice open support for BRICS’ long standing policy of promoting intra-BRICS trade in local currencies. He may maintain ambivalence, even though in his address to the 2015 BRICS Summit in Russia, he said: “We have to work and fight together as one.” But that was before Trump! Even the laying of under-sea gas pipeline from Oman to Gujarat may not be fast tracked. Despite US attack on ships carrying Indian sailors, Modi Govt will not stall negotiations for a trade deal with US. Modi Govt will maintain a non-confrontational and accommodating posture towards Trump despite statements of open thuggery like the one given by US State Department on 13th June which justified the attack on the three ships manned by Indian crew because of ‘violations of the US blockade and the illicit transport of Iranian oil’. He is expected to voice support for respecting sovereignty of countries, unhindered navigation, and peaceful resolution of all issues. But he will not voice support for free flow of Iranian oil, which is the sine qua non of sovereignty.”
I reiterate these long positions.

Assembly Economy
The technologically advanced countries, including China and Russia, admire our technological weakness which is at the center of our accommodating and compromising positions. We know that technology is today’s principal currency of power, but we don’t want to climb the Everest of Technology, so to speak. We want to be the porters for the technology climbers: principally US, China, Russia, EU, Japan and South Korea. In Porter’s language, in this tech led world we lead neither in cost nor in differentiation, our focus is on harnessing our vast pool of low and intermediate level technological manpower for an assembly economy, and for services.   We cannot fabricate semi-conductor chips, and we do not want to go in for long term expensive pursuit of developing chip making technology by setting up dedicated technology clusters. Instead, we are on bended knees to export our top technological pool.  At home we want to offer abundant fresh water and low/intermediate level technological manpower to foreign owned and controlled chip fabs to fabricate chips which we as Vishvguru will advertise as our chip manufacturing centers.  We cannot manufacture high end passenger aircrafts parts, components, and subsystems. But we will offer abundant land and manpower resources to Boeing to set up repair and maintenance facilities for their aircrafts, and allow them to dump millions of tons of metal scrap in our remote wilderness. We cannot mass produce high end computers using our own components. But we can offered our land, water, electricity and base level technological manpower to set up foreign owned and controlled data banks (using India assembled computers) even if it leads to desertification of our lands and constant noise pollution in our cities. We know about Chinese aggression in Eastern Ladakh. But in view of the alarming fall in FDI and FPI we are now open to Chinese investments in vast sectors of our economy ranging from electronics, energy, industrial equipment and pharmaceutical industries that would be co-owned and controlled by China. We will continue to spend lacs of crores on social engineering pivoted on subsidized food to 80% of our population for the political survival of parties in power, but won’t increase our R&D expenditure from the abysmal ~0.7% of GDP. Instead, we will gradually put up our technology jewels – ISRO, DRDO, and eventually even BARC – for sale to the highest bidder in our private sector as we march into comparative technological oblivion.

The Forex Hunger 
We are facing not only substantial current account deficit year after year, we are now on the cusp of facing reverse capital outflow. We need forex from the Indian diaspora especially in the US, which accounts for ~30% of forex remittances. But that is not enough. So, we've gone for special FCNR(B) deposits from NRIs at rates of interest between 5.5-7%. In foreign currency! And that too tax free! We have beaten annual yields of 10 year US Treasury notes that offer 4.95% (as on 10th Sept) yield which is taxable. We are in survival mode, but we want to tell the world (possibly at the BRICS Summit) how strong we are because of our  huge forex reserves, that will endlessly pay for our imports and service our massive forex borrowings. We have abandoned the quest for technological excellence and we are readily embracing the colonization of our economy as an existential necessity. Our vision of Viksit Bharat is to become the largest assembly and services economy of the world.
We are expected to diplomatically echo these positions in the BRICS Summit this weekend. The applause from our guests should be deafening.
